    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The religion centred on sacrifices and fire rituals recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, among them the Agnihotra, the Soma rituals and the Ashvamedha, the horse sacrifice, offered to deities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, Agni and Varuna.</li><li>Ancestor worship was an important, perhaps the central, part of the early religion, which mentions an afterlife but not the later doctrines of karma and reincarnation.</li><li>In the late Vedic period it developed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a>, which gave great weight to the priestly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> class and helped to shape contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, though the two differ significantly.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The oldest inscriptions in Old Indic, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rig Veda</a>, were found in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> kings took Old Indic throne names and used the term ṛta, meaning cosmic order and truth.</p></aside>
    </section>

