    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The historical Vedic religion, also called Vedism or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a>, was the religion of some Indo-Aryan peoples of the northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>) during the Vedic period, c. 1500–500 BCE. Its roots lie in the Indo-Iranian religion of the Sintashta (c. 2200–1750 BCE) and Andronovo (c. 2000–1150 BCE) cultures of the Eurasian Steppe. According to Anthony, the Old Indic religion probably emerged among Indo-European immigrants in the contact zone between the Zeravshan River and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, as a syncretic mixture of old Central Asian and new Indo-European elements. It borrowed distinctive beliefs and practices from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> culture, supported by at least 383 non-Indo-European words, among them the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and the ritual drink soma.</p><p>The religion then developed in the northwest during the early Vedic period (c. 1500–1100 BCE), influenced by the remnants of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>; White (2003) cites three scholars who have emphatically demonstrated that it partly derives from that civilisation. After c. 1100 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a> moved into the Gangetic Plain and became settled farmers, further merging with native cultures, and in the late Vedic period (c. 1100–500 BCE) Brahmanism developed out of it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vedas.jpg" alt="Vedas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda,_manuscript_page_sample_i,_Mandala_1,_Hymn_1_(Sukta_1),_Adhyaya_1,_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ms Sarah Welch, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ancient Vedic religion has been described as a complex animistic religion with polytheistic and pantheistic aspects. Ancestor worship was an important, maybe the central, component, with rites such as sraddha, the offering of food to the ancestors. The early layers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> do not mention karma and rebirth but do mention belief in an afterlife; reincarnation, or saṃsāra, is absent from early texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, and its roots lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> of the late Vedic period.</p><p>The Rigveda counts 33 devas, eleven each of earth, space and heaven. The pantheon knows two classes, Devas and Asuras; the Devas, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>, Varuna, Aryaman and Bhaga, are deities of cosmic and social order. Most notable are the heroic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, Agni, the sacrificial fire and messenger of the gods, and Soma, the deified sacred drink. A central myth, Rig Veda 1.32, tells how Indra drinks Soma and slays the dragon Vritra, freeing the rivers, the cows and Dawn. Ethics rest on concepts such as ṛta, fixed or settled order and divine law, and satya, which came to mean truthfulness in thought, speech and action.</p><p>Rites include fire rituals with oblations (havir), such as the Agnihotra, the Soma rituals, the royal consecration (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasuya">Rajasuya</a>) and the Ashvamedha. Burial and cremation are both seen from the Rigvedic period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya.jpg" alt="Yajnavalkya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yajnavalkya_and_Janaka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">எஸ். பி. கிருஷ்ணமூர்த்தி, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The texts of the Vedic period, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a>, are mainly the four Vedic Samhitas, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, Aranyakas and some of the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> also placed in it. Michael Witzel states that the Vedic canon consists of all the texts that originated in and were used by the Vedic schools. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> record the liturgy connected with the rituals and sacrifices. They were at first preserved only by oral transmission, and the earliest recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, dates from the 11th century. They are also part of the scripture of contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> is a collection of hymns to various deities, and according to Anthony <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> was the subject of 250 of them, a quarter of the whole. The later layers of the Vedic literature, the Aranyakas and Upanishads, show a different soteriology based on reincarnation and little concern with ancestor rites, and they begin to interpret the earlier rituals philosophically. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a> (c. 800 BCE) discusses the earliest versions of the karma doctrine. In the Hindu tradition the revered sages of this era include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a>, Atri, Bharadvaja, Vasistha, Vishwamitra and Kanva.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The religion existed in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> in the early Vedic period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley in multiple waves during the 2nd millennium BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a> took shape in the Ganges basin around c. 1000 BCE. Its emphasis on ritual and the dominant position of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> developed as an ideology in the Kuru-Pancala realm, and it expanded over a wider area after that realm's demise and its incorporation into the Magadha-based empires. It co-existed with local religions such as the Yaksha cults. The eastern Ganges plain was dominated by another Indo-Aryan complex, which rejected the later Brahmanical ideology and gave rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p><p>Brahmanism declined in the post-Vedic period of the Second Urbanisation. The Shramanic movement, the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, the Nanda and Mauryan empires, and foreign rule in the northwest threatened the income and patronage of rural Brahmins. This was overcome by providing new services and incorporating the non-Vedic Indo-Aryan heritage of the eastern Gangetic plain and local traditions, which gave rise to contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. This new Brahmanism appealed to rulers and dominated Indian society from the classical age of Hinduism in the early centuries CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a> is loosely so named, in Heesterman's words, because of the religious and legal importance it places on the brāhmaṇa, the priestly class. The Vedic texts were preserved into modern times by the different priestly schools. Post-Vedic texts such as the Dharmasutras and Dharmaśāstras gave prominence to the priestly (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of society.</p><p>In the period 800–200 BCE, which Muesse calls the classical period, traditional religious practices and beliefs were reassessed, and the Brahmins and the rituals they performed no longer enjoyed the same prestige as in the Vedic period. Michaels calls 500–200 BCE a time of ascetic reformism. The non-Vedic śramaṇa traditions existed alongside Brahmanism: they were not direct outgrowths of Vedism but movements with mutual influences with Brahmanical traditions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> evolved out of them. According to Olivelle, some scholars hold that the renouncer tradition was an organic and logical development of Vedic ideas, while others hold that it emerged from the indigenous non-Aryan population; the debate is longstanding and ongoing.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/upanishads"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upanishads.jpg" alt="Upanishads" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_and_DiscipleI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The historical Vedic religion is now generally accepted as a predecessor of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, though the two are not the same: the textual evidence shows differences, such as belief in an afterlife in place of the later reincarnation and saṃsāra. According to Michaels, the Vedic gods declined but did not disappear, local cults were assimilated into the pantheon, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> became more prominent, giving rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> gradually evolved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>, which considers itself the goal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, while Mīmāṃsā philosophers argued that the gods have no existence apart from the mantras that name them.</p><p>Some practice continues. According to David Knipe, some communities preserve portions of the religion, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, where the Nambudiri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> continue the Śrauta rituals. The Kalash people of northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> practise a form of it, though Witzel notes differences, such as fire placed beside the altar instead of in it. In the 19th century western publications held the Vedic religion to be unrelated to Hinduism, while reform movements such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmo-samaj">Brahmo Samaj</a> and Neo-Vedanta urged a return to the Vedas.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The historical Vedic religion was the religion of some Indo-Aryan peoples of the northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> from c. 1500 to 500 BCE, centred on sacrifices to gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and Agni recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. It developed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a> and is now generally seen as a predecessor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>.</em></p>

